    Added two new command-line options to improve performance with preemption support (#327)
    
    The new options:
    1. -mshave-low-impact-preemption which produces a modified control flow for the preemption checks. Rather than placing the BRU.SWP and loop bounds re-evaluation into a new block following the loop, they are now placed into a separate block at the end of the function. The block following the loop now simply checks the preemption signal and jumps to the new block at the end of the function. This means that the cost is always 7-cycles whether the preemption is triggered or not.
    2. -mshave-preemption-max-loop-depth=<int> which specifies a maximum loop depth at which preemption checks should be placed. The depth is measured from the inside out for nested loops, so a value of 1 in this option means only the inner-most loops in a file should get the preemption checks.
    
    * Reorganising control-flow for preemption so that there are fewer instructions on the critical path
    Preemption handling is now in its own basic-block at the end of the function, so in theory this shouldn't affect the instruction cache either
    
    * Adding new options to allow control over using the "low impact" version of preemption, and setting the max loop depth for preemption checks
    
    * Fixing insert point
    
    * Fixing doc
    
    * Missed a - in the documentation

