commit 7fa5311081c5cfba620301e9afc18a73893237ff
Author: Ferran Pallarès Roca <ferran.pallares.roca@intel.com>
Date:   Tue Apr 26 15:01:42 2022 +0100

    Try to make multithreaded Windows builds more responsive
    
    Previous to this patch we were using '-maxcpucount' (-m) switch to restrict CPU
    usage. This sets the number of MSBuild.exe processes that may run in parallel,
    that is the number of _projects_ that can be built simultaneously. The problem
    is that each MSBuild.exe will spawn several cl.exe processes, likely causing
    oversubscription in the current configuration.
    
    'CL_MPCount' property set by Visual Studio will control how many cl.exe
    processes are spawned for each MSBuild.exe, so we can have finer control by
    setting '-maxcpucount=1' and tuning this property instead.
    
    An even better approach would be to use MTT for a seamless control of the
    number of processes used across multiple projects and MSBuild.exe instances,
    but that is only supported starting with VS 2019 16.3. See
    https://devblogs.microsoft.com/cppblog/improved-parallelism-in-msbuild/ for
    details.
    
    In addition, passing -low option causes MSBuild.exe to run at low process
    priority, making the system more responsive while still using available CPU
    resources.

