    Fix for bug #29510 - Pass the current optimisation level through to the instruction selector
    The optimising instruction scheduler has been enabled at -O0 since the upgrade to LLVM 5.0 due to a sneaky default argument that we missed when constructing the instruction selector.
    The instruction selector allows the optimisation level in the target machine to be temporarily changed for a particular function. It saves the original optimisation level before changing, using the value set in the instruction selector itself. Since we weren't initialising the optimisation level ourselves, the "saved" level was the default argument in the instruction selector constructor (-O2). As a result, the "restored" optimisation level in the target machine was incorrect.

