    When updating the starting cycle of anti-dependent successors of the node just scheduled, the post-RA scheduler will now take the successor's latency into account and offset by its latency plus the predecessor's latency. For anti-dependent nodes, the successor just needs to read its operands before the predecessor writes back, it does not need to finish execution before the write-back (previous behaviour).
    Call nodes are a special case for this, and the previous behaviour apply for them.
    
